  "status": "published",
  "title": "She Went Missing 101 Times in British Care. How Would Texas Have Handled Her Case?",
  "deck": "At 15, she went through an Islamic wedding ceremony with a man later convicted of abusing her. Her social worker attended. Texas law would bar the marriage, but would authorities have acted on the same warnings?",
  "category": "investigations",
  "categoryLabel": "INVESTIGATIONS",
  "type": "INVESTIGATION",
  "author": "Epic Perspective News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-27",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-27",
  "image": {
    "src": "assets/images/bradford-foster-care-texas-20261127.jpg",
    "alt": "AI-generated editorial illustration of a Bradford-inspired street at dusk, with the headline She Went Missing 101 Times.",
    "credit": "AI-generated editorial illustration / EP News"
  },
  "body": [
    "Raja Zulqurnean told a 15-year-old girl in a Bradford children’s home that he wanted a wife. Prosecutors said he told her to dress modestly and wear Asian clothing. When the two went through an Islamic wedding ceremony, the social worker assigned to the girl attended. Prosecutors said police were told about the ceremony. They found no sign that it was investigated.",
    "Police call the girl Anna to protect her identity. She was 13 when the abuse began. Police records cited in court show she went missing 101 times between 2002 and 2004. A social worker testified that men arrived at the children’s home in as many as 10 cars a night and that staff passed license plate numbers to police weekly. Staff had already raised concerns that Anna was being sexually exploited.",
    "Anna reported the abuse in 2019. Four more men were sentenced on November 23, bringing the number convicted of offenses against her to 13. Mohammed Baig received 21 years for three rapes and two indecent assaults. Two others received prison terms and one received a suspended sentence. Zulqurnean’s sentence in the earlier proceedings was increased to 23 years on appeal.",
    "## The ceremony and the men’s backgrounds",
    "Zulqurnean’s demands about Anna’s clothing and his claim that he wanted a wife were part of the prosecution’s account of how he treated her. Anna told the court she had been married to an abuser and held social services responsible for enabling it. Bradford and neighboring Keighley are now part of Britain’s statutory inquiry into grooming gangs. One question the case leaves open is what Anna’s social worker knew about the concerns at the home when she attended the ceremony.",
    "All four men sentenced this week identified themselves to police as Asian Pakistani. Baroness Louise Casey’s 2025 audit found that men of Asian ethnicity were overrepresented among suspects in group-based child sexual exploitation in three police-force areas it examined. The audit called for more research into why men of Asian and Pakistani ethnicity were overrepresented in some areas and whether cultural factors influenced how groups operated. National records lacked ethnicity data for roughly two-thirds of perpetrators, so the audit could not establish a reliable national profile. The British government has since commissioned research that includes offenders’ ethnicity, religion and motivations.",
    "In the United States, the share of adults identifying as Muslim rose from 0.4% in 2007 to 1.2% in 2023–24, according to Pew Research Center. Those figures measure religion. The British audit examined ethnicity in local police records. The two sources cannot tell us whether the growth of America’s Muslim population affects the risk of this kind of exploitation. In Bradford, the evidence is specific to Zulqurnean and what he did. He presented a child in government care as his wife, and her social worker was there to see it.",
    "## Could it happen in Texas?",
    "An adult Muslim man could not legally marry a 15-year-old in Texas through an Islamic ceremony. Texas generally requires marriage applicants to be 18. The exception for minors requires a qualifying court order granting emancipation, and a 15-year-old is too young to obtain one from a Texas court. Parental permission and a social worker’s presence cannot make the marriage valid.",
    "Texas makes it a third-degree felony to knowingly conduct a marriage ceremony for a minor whose marriage is prohibited by law. That provision applies to the person conducting the ceremony. The adult claiming to be the child’s husband could not use an invalid marriage to claim the spousal defense under Texas’s sexual assault statute. A Texas social worker with reasonable cause to suspect child abuse must personally report it within 24 hours.",
    "England and Wales already barred legal marriage below 16 when Anna’s ceremony took place. In 2023, they raised the minimum age to 18 and made it a crime to take steps intended to cause a child to marry, including through a ceremony with no legal effect. Anna’s case shows how much still depends on what police and social workers do when someone tells them a ceremony has taken place.",
    "## Who would put the reports together?",
    "Federal law requires state agencies to report a child missing from foster care immediately, and within 24 hours at the latest, to both law enforcement and the National Center for Missing & Exploited Children. States must also have procedures to find out what happened while the child was away, including screening for possible sex trafficking after the child returns.",
    "A 2023 federal inspector general audit reviewed 100 missing episodes. Only 33 were reported to the national center on time. Twenty-two were reported late, and 45 were never reported to the center. The audit tracked whether the reports reached the center. It did not establish what happened to the children while they were missing.",
    "Texas publishes data on children missing from care. To see how it would handle a case like Anna’s, start with the children who went missing more than once. How many episodes were reported on time? How many children were screened for trafficking when they came back? If the same adult or vehicle appears in reports from two counties, who is responsible for noticing? Casey’s audit found that even records held within a single British police force could sit in systems that did not communicate.",
    "The test for Texas starts with the second disappearance. When a child comes back, does someone sit down with her privately, pull the earlier reports, and find out who she was with before she goes missing again?"
  ],
